      return (
        <div role="alert" style={{ padding: 16 }}>
          <h2>Something went wrong.</h2>
          <pre>{error.message}</pre>
          <button type="button" onClick={this.retry}>
            Retry
          </button>
        </div>
      );
    }

    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

Usage:

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary
  logError={(error, info) => {
    // Send to Sentry, Datadog, LogRocket, etc.
    console.error({
      message: error.message,
      stack: error.stack,
      componentStack: info.componentStack,
    });
  }}
>
  <App />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Custom fallback:

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary
  fallback={({ error, retry }) => (
    <section role="alert">
      <h1>Failed to render page</h1>
      <p>{error.message}</p>
      <button onClick={retry}>Try again</button>
    </section>
  )}
>
  <Dashboard />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Notes: React error boundaries catch render/lifecycle errors below them. They do not catch async errors, event handler errors, server errors, or errors thrown inside the boundary itself.
